; reflect — the fix step. Given the plan and everything the gates found (funk
; check errors, style violations, the connection review), produce a CORRECTED
; plan in the same JSON shape. This closes the loop: generate → gate → reflect →
; regenerate. It fixes the CODE; failures that are funk's own fault are not its
; job — `diagnoseFunk` routes those to a ticket instead.
package "funk/forge" {
  version 0.1.0
}

fn reflect {
  name "Reflect"
  doc """
    # Reflect
    ### Revise a plan to fix the check errors, style violations and connection issues.
  """
  examples """
    # Examples:
    (reflect task plan "mean.funk: unknown function \"/\"" "") → a plan using maths.div
    (reflect task plan "" "sub(a,b): args in wrong order → (maths.sub b a)") → swapped args fixed
  """
  in  (task Str)
      (plan Json (doc "Current Project Plan"))
      (errors Str (doc "Check And Style Problems"))
      (review Str (doc "Connection Problems"))
  out (revised Json (doc "Corrected Project Plan"))
  engine claude
  src """
    You are fixing a funk project. You get the TASK, the current PLAN (JSON), the
    check + style ERRORS, and the connection REVIEW. Produce a CORRECTED plan in the
    EXACT same JSON shape:
    { "header", "functions": [ {name,kind,title,doc,examples,in,out,engine,src,
      requires,needs,effects} ] }.

    Fix EVERY error, style violation and connection problem. Keep what already works.
    Reuse the same package name in "header".

    funk correctness (the usual mistakes):
    - funk has NO infix operators. NEVER (/ a b), (+ a b), (* a b), (> a b).
      Arithmetic: maths.add, maths.sub, maths.mul, maths.div. Comparison:
      maths.lt, maths.lte, maths.gt, maths.gte, maths.eq, maths.neq.
    - A node's children are its arguments IN ORDER — fix swapped/misordered args
      (e.g. a range is (maths.sub (max …) (min …)), NOT min − max).
    - An input that never reaches the output, or a helper whose result is discarded,
      is a bug — wire it in or remove it.
    - A COMPOSITE (engine "") cannot read struct fields — `birth.name` FAILS. If a
      body needs a field, add a python atom that extracts it and call that instead.
    - SIGNATURE MISMATCH ("X expects N input(s), got M"): make the callee's `in`
      match the call. A call `(f a b)` needs f to declare exactly two NAMED input
      ports; fix EITHER the ports OR the call so the arg count matches. An
      "unknown identifier" in a body means a name that is neither a let binding nor
      an input port — add the missing input port (or let), or remove the reference.
    - funk has NO RECURSION: if a composite calls ITSELF, rewrite it with `while`
      (a stateful bounded loop) or a fixed chain — a self-call never resolves.
    - PRUNE DEAD CODE. If the error is in a function NOT reachable from `main`
      (nothing calls it — often an OLD approach you already replaced), DELETE that
      function from "functions" entirely instead of trying to fix it. Never leave
      two implementations of the same step; keep the ONE the main body uses.
    - Declare EVERY package a body calls in "header" with a `use` line
      (e.g. use "funk/std/maths" as maths).
    - "engine":"python" → a python body that returns (the ONLY place for return, IO,
      struct-as-dict). "engine":"claude" → the "src" IS a natural-language PROMPT
      (interpret/write/verify). "engine":"" → a composite funk body emitting with
      flush into the OUTPUT PORT NAME (no python, no return).
    - Streams: an output typed "(Stream T)" for fan-out. The reactive lift is
      AUTOMATIC — there is NO `lift` function; a scalar function applied to a stream
      fires per item, so just call it: (perItem (producer …)). collect gathers the
      resulting stream into a List. (An "unknown function lift" error means someone
      wrote `lift` — remove it and apply the function directly.) A step that must
      wait for SEVERAL inputs (zip barrier) simply takes them all as inputs.
    - Capabilities: declare "requires" (pip deps), "effects" (fs/net IO) and "needs"
      (config) on the python atom that does the IO — "" elsewhere.
    - Exactly one function has "kind":"main"; others use "lib"/"skill"/"gate"/"type".
    - Types are Num Str Bool List Json Time Any Stream Fn; domain schemas are "type"
      functions passed around as Json (only python reads their fields).

    house STYLE — every function MUST have all of these:
    - "title": a Capitalised display name.
    - "doc": markdown, "# <Title>" then "### <One capitalised sentence, ending with a
      period.>".
    - "examples": markdown, "# Examples:" then one or more "<call> → <result>".
    - EVERY port MUST be "(name Type (doc \"…\"))" — the port NAME comes FIRST and is
      MANDATORY (camelCase, DESCRIPTIVE — NEVER single letters like r/x). A port
      without a name makes the body's references unbound.
      GOOD:  in (numbers (List Num) (doc \"Numbers To Sum\"))
      WRONG: in (List Num) (doc \"Numbers To Sum\")  — no port name; a body using
      `numbers` cannot bind to it.
    - A port's OWN doc (the "(doc \"…\")" on an in/out port) is a short Title-Case
      label — single line, NO markdown, NO trailing period (e.g. "Derived Connection
      Trees", not "the derived connection trees, from projectGraph").
    - title/doc/examples in the SAME LANGUAGE as the task.

    Output ONLY the corrected JSON. No prose, no fences.
  """
}
